          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            There are at least two things that are called
            %em quantum anomalies
            in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong> anomalous action functional
                \: the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
                (in
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral quantization
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
                on
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space"} configuration space
            %li
              %p
                %strong anomalous symmetry
                (gauge anomaly): a symmetry of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
                does not extend to a symmetry of the exponentiated action times the path integral measure; or equivalently the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                of a group on classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
                is not preserved by
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#AnomalousActionFunctional Anomalous action functional
          %p
            There are two major kinds of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
            that may be anomalous in that they are not actually
            = succeed "s/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonlinear+functional"} functional
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                theories with fermions (see e.g.
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinors+in+Yang-Mills+theory"} spinors in Yang-Mills theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
                is given by a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operator"} Fredholm operator
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theories
                with higher degree gauge fields (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cocycles
                of higher degree.)
          %h4#fermionic_anomalies Fermionic anomalies
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            can be decomposed into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermionic+path+integral"} fermionic path integral
            (see there for more details) over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermionic+fields"} fermionic fields
            followed by that over the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+fields"} bosonic fields
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berezin+integral"} Berezin integral
            = succeed "," do
              %em twisted function
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            called a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant+line+bundle"} determinant line bundle
            or, in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 8
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 8k+2
            dimensions, its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+root"} square root
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pfaffian+line+bundle"} Pfaffian line bundle
          %p
            So to even start making sense of the remaining path integral over the bosonic degree of freedom, this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant+line+bundle"} determinant line bundle
            or the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pfaffian+line+bundle"} Pfaffian line bundle
            has to be trivializable. Its non-trivializability is the
            %em fermionic anomaly
            \.
          %p
            More in detail (
            %a{:href => "#Freed86"}> Freed 86
            ), the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            over an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\overline \phi, D \phi)
            for
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  D \;\colon\; V \longrightarrow W
          %p
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operator"} Fredholm operator
            computes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant"} determinant
            of that operator. Formally this is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant+line+bundle"} determinant line bundle
            over the remaining
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi det
                  %mi V
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi det
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi det
                  %mi ker
                  %mi D
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi det
                  %mi coker
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (det V)^\ast \otimes (det W)
                  \\simeq
                  (det ker D)^\ast \otimes (det coker D)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the left hand side makes sense and the equivalence holds for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            finite dimensional, and where the right hand side is the definition of the expression for general
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fredholm+operators"} Fredholm operators
            = succeed "))" do
              %a{:href => "#Freed86"} Freed 86, 1.
          %p
            In more detail this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant+line+bundle"} determinant line bundle
            also carries a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection"} connection
            on a bundle. To make the formal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            of this bundle, into an actual function, one this bundle with connection needs to be trivializable and trivialized. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/obstruction"} obstruction
            to this is the anomaly.
          %h4#higher_gaugetheoretic_anomalies Higher gauge-theoretic anomalies
          %p
            For the moment see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+mechanism"} Green-Schwarz mechanism
            for more.
          %h3#AnomalousSymmetry Anomalous symmetry
          %blockquote
            %p under construction
          %p Let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S : C \to \mathbb{R}
          %p
            be a (well defined)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            for its resolved
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-geometry"} dg-geometry
            and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi BV
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S^{BV} : P \to \mathbb{R}
          %p
            for the BV-action functional, both as given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST-BV+formalism"} BRST-BV formalism
          %p
            If the action functional is local (comes from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            on a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            a priori only carries a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+structure"} presymplectic structure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi BV
                  %mo :
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\Psi^{BV} : P \to \mathbb{R}
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi S
                    %mi Ψ
                    %mi BV
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\Psi^{BV}
            does induce a genuine
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+structure"} symplectic structure
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived
            space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p
            For ordinary
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson manifold
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maxim+Kontsevich"} Maxim Kontsevich
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+quantization"} deformation quantization
            always exist. But if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is in general a nontrivial derived
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Lie+algebroid"} infinity-Lie algebroid
            (its function algebra has “ghosts” and “ghosts of ghost”: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chevalley-Eilenberg+algebra"} Chevalley-Eilenberg algebra
            generators) and the theorem does not apply. Instead, the quantization of the derived symplectic space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            exists only if the first and second
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Lie+algebroid+cohomology"} infinity-Lie algebroid cohomology
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            vanishes:
          %p These two cohomology groups
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Anom
                    %mi gauge
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊕
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi CE
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Anom_{gauge} = H^1(CE(P)) \oplus H^2(CE(P))
          %p
            are called the
            %strong gauge anomaly
            of the system. Only if they vanish does the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            encoded by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            exist.
          %p
            More concretely, the function algebra on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a graded-commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            equipped with a graded Poisson bracket
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mi BV
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{-,-\}_{BV}
            and an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q \in C^\infty(P)
            (the BV-BRST charge) whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+vector+field"} Hamiltonian vector field
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
            that is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
            of the dg-algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(P)
            to a non commutative graded algebra with commutator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-]
            will exist, it may happen that the image
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            under the quantization no longer satisfies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+master+equation"} quantum master equation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℏ
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [S,S] = \hbar \Delta S
          %p
            Therefore the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivation"} derivation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [S,-]
            will not define a quantized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
            and therefore the quantization of the graded-commutative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(P)
            will only be a noncommutative algebra, not a non-commutative dg-algebra, hence will not be functions on a non-commutative space in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+geometry"} derived geometry
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#anomalous_action_functional_2 Anomalous action functional
          %h4#SpinningParticlesAndSuperBranes Spinning particles and super-branes
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-model"} sigma-model
            for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetric
            fundamental
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"} brane
            on a target space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has an anomaly coming from the nontriviality of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pfaffian+line+bundle"} Pfaffian line bundle
            = succeed "ic" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has a sufficiently high lift through the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+tower"} Whitehead tower
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(n)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong Spin structure
                the worldline anomaly for the spinning particle/superparticle vanishes when
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                has
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin+structure"} Spin structure
              %p This is a classical result. A concrete derivation is in
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                  ,
                  %em Global anomalies in String theory
                  in
                  %em Symposium on anomalies, geometry, topology
                  , World Scientific Publishing, Singapore (1985)
            %li
              %p
                %strong String structure
                the worldsheet anomaly for the spinning string/superstring in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
                vanishes (essentially) when
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                has
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+structure"} String structure
              %p
                This is originally due to Killingback and Witten. A commented list of literature is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/string/archives/000572.html"} here
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrich+Bunke"} Ulrich Bunke
                gave the rigorous proof
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrich+Bunke"}> Ulrich Bunke
                  ,
                  %em String structures and trivialisations of a Pfaffian line bundle
                  (
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0909.0846"}> arXiv
                  )
          %p
            in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            in general and
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+string+structure"} differential string structure
          %ul
            %li
              %strong Fivebrane structure
              the worldvolume anomaly for the super-5-brane in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+heterotic+string+theory"} dual heterotic string theory
              vanishes (essentially) when
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              has
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fivebrane+structure"} Fivebrane structure
          %h4#gravitational_anomaly Gravitational anomaly
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitational+anomaly"} gravitational anomaly
          %h4#axial_anomaly Axial anomaly
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axial+anomaly"} axial anomaly
          %h4#conformal_anomaly_of_the_string Conformal anomaly of the string
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldsheet"} worldsheet
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosonic+string"} bosonic string
            (in flat space, without further background fields) has an anomaly unless the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/target+space"} target space
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo =
                    %mn 26
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 26
          %p
            This is discussed as a condition of trivialization of a bundle in (
            %a{:href => "#Freed86"}> Freed 86, section 2
            ). A brief summary is stated
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/a/99667/381"} this comment on MO
          %p
            For more see at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+anomaly"} conformal anomaly
            for more.
          %h4#freedwitten_anomaly Freed-Witten anomaly
          %p
            see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Freed-Witten+anomaly"} Freed-Witten anomaly
          %h4#diaconescumoorewitten_anomaly Diaconescu-Moore-Witten anomaly
          %p
            see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Diaconescu-Moore-Witten+anomaly"} Diaconescu-Moore-Witten anomaly
          %h4#m5brane_anomaly M5-Brane anomaly
          %p
            see at
            %em
              %a{:href => "M5-brane#AnomalyCancellation"} M5-brane anomaly
          %h3#anomalous_symmetry_2 Anomalous symmetry
          %h4#conformal_anomaly Conformal anomaly
          %p
            For the moment see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liouville+cocycle"} Liouville cocycle
          %h3#other Other
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field+tadpole+cancellation"} RR-field tadpole cancellation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-field+tadpole+cancellation"} C-field tadpole cancellation
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+anomaly"} classical anomaly
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%27t+Hooft+anomaly"} 't Hooft anomaly
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles+in+physics"} fiber bundles in physics
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#AnomalousActionFunction"} References on anomalous action functionals
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#ReferencesGaugeAnomaly"} References on gauge anomalies
          %p Anomalies originate in the 1949 article by Steinberger:
          %ul
            %li
              J. Steinberger,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Use of Subtraction Fields and the Lifetimes of Some Types of Meson Decay
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.76.1180"} doi
          %p Another early reference is
          %ul
            %li
              Stephen L. Adler,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Axial-Vector Vertex in Spinor Electrodynamics
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRev.177.2426"} doi
          %h3#AnomalousActionFunction Anomalous action functional
          %p The original articles on anomalous action functionals are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luis+Alvarez-Gaum%C3%A9"}> Luis Alvarez-Gaumé
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Gravitational Anomalies
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(84)90066-X"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(84)90066-X
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/192309"} spire:192309
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luis+Alvarez-Gaum%C3%A9"}> Luis Alvarez-Gaumé
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Ginsparg"} Paul Ginsparg
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The structure of gauge and gravitational anomalies
                %strong 161
                (1985) 423 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0003-4916(85)90087-9"}> doi:10.1016/0003-4916(85)90087-9
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/202565"} spire:202565
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Global gravitational anomalies
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01212448"} doi:10.1007/BF01212448
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1103943444"} euclid:1103943444
          %p Review
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paolo+Di+Vecchia"}> Paolo Di Vecchia
                ,
                %em Green-Schwarz anomaly cancellation
                (2010) (slides
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/DiVecchiaAnomaly.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeffrey+Harvey"}> Jeffrey Harvey
                ,
                %em TASI 2003 Lectures on Anomalies
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0509097"}> arXiv:hep-th/0509097
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/692082"} spire:692082
          %p
            The mathematical formulation of this in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+theory"} index theory
            is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"}> Michael Atiyah
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isadore+Singer"} Isadore Singer
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Dirac operators coupled to vector potentials
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 81
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.81.8.2597"} doi:10.1073/pnas.81.8.2597
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/23378"} jstor:23378
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Michel+Bismut"}> Jean-Michel Bismut
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"} Daniel Freed
                %em The analysis of elliptic families. I. Metrics and connections on determinant bundles
                , Comm. Math. Phys. 106 (1986), no. 1, 159–176 (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01210930"}> doi:10.1007/BF01210930
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104115586"} euclid:1104115586
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Michel+Bismut"}> Jean-Michel Bismut
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"} Daniel Freed
                %em The analysis of elliptic families. II. Dirac operators, eta invariants, and the holonomy theorem
                , Comm. Math. Phys. 107 (1986), no. 1, 103–163 (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01206955"}> doi:10.1007/BF01206955
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104115934"} euclid:1104115934
          %p and a clear comprehensive account of the situation (topological anomaly, geometric anomaly) is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raphael+Flauger"}> Raphael Flauger
                ,
                %em Anomalies and the Atiyah-Singer Index Theorem
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/FlaugerAnomalies.pdf", :title => "pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Freed86
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Determinants, torsion, and strings
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01221001"} doi:10.1007/BF01221001
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.cmp/1104116145"} euclid:1104116145
          %p
            Slick formulation of these anomalies as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invertible+topological+field+theories"} invertible topological field theories
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Anomalies and Invertible Field Theories
              %a{:href => "http://scgp.stonybrook.edu/events/event-pages/string-math-2013"} StringMath2013
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.7224"}> arXiv.1404.7224
              )
          %p A physicists’ monograph is
          %ul
            %li
              Reinhold A. Bertlmann,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Anomalies in quantum field theory
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luis+Alvarez-Gaum%C3%A9"}> Luis Alvarez-Gaumé
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Miguel+A.+V%C3%A1zquez-Mozo"} Miguel A. Vázquez-Mozo
              = succeed "," do
                %em Anomalies and the Green-Schwarz Mechanism
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Quantum+Gravity"} Handbook of Quantum Gravity
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.06467"} arXiv:2211.06467
          %p A clear description of the quantum anomalies for higher gauge theories is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization+and+generalized+differential+cohomology"} Dirac charge quantization and generalized differential cohomology
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0011220"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            As an application of this, a detailed discussion of the cancellation of the anomaly of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            in 11-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Freed"}> Dan Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Greg+Moore"} Greg Moore
              %em Setting the quantum integrand of M-theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0409135"}> arXiv:hep-th/0409135
              )
          %p
            The role of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+structure"} spin structure
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              %em Global anomalies in String theory
              in
              %em Symposium on anomalies, geometry, topology
              , World Scientific Publishing, Singapore (1985)
          %h3#ReferencesGaugeAnomaly Gauge anomaly
          %p
            The original work on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chiral+anomaly"} chiral anomaly
            is due to
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+Adler"}> Stephen Adler
                \.
                %em Axial-Vector Vertex in Spinor Electrodynamics
                Physical Review 177 (5): 2426. (1969)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Bell"}> John Bell
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roman+Jackiw"} Roman Jackiw
                %em A PCAC puzzle: π0→γγ in the σ-model“. Il Nuovo Cimento A 60: 47. (1969)
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %p L. Faddeev and S. Shatashvili, “Algebraic and Hamiltonian Methods in the theory of Nonabelian Anomalies,” Theor. Math. Fiz., 60 (1984) 206; english transl. Theor. Math. Phys. 60 (1984) 770.
            %li
              %p B. Zumino, “Chiral anomalies and differential geometry,” in Relativity, Groups and Topology II, proceedings of the Les Houches summer school, B.S. DeWitt and R. Stora, eds. North-Holland, 1984.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Percacci"}> Roberto Percacci
                , section 6 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Non-Perturbative Quantum Field Theory – An Introduction to Topological and Semiclassical Methods
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.22323/9788898587056"} doi:10.22323/9788898587056
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/handle/20.500.12657/96025/9788898587056.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikio+Nakahara"}> Mikio Nakahara
                , Chapter 13 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geometry%2C+Topology+and+Physics"} Geometry, Topology and Physics
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1201/9781315275826"} doi:10.1201/9781315275826
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://alpha.sinp.msu.ru/~panov/LibBooks/GRAV/(Graduate_Student_Series_in_Physics)Mikio_Nakahara-Geometry,_Topology_and_Physics,_Second_Edition_(Graduate_Student_Series_in_Physics)-Institute_of_Physics_Publishing(2003).pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Loriano+Bonora"}> Loriano Bonora
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fermions and Anomalies in Quantum Field Theories
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-21928-3"} doi:10.1007/978-3-031-21928-3
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Loriano+Bonora"}> Loriano Bonora
                , Stefano G. Giaccari:
                %em Something Anomalies can tell about SM and Gravity
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.07470"}> arXiv:2412.07470
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %h4#in_bvbrst_formulation In BV-BRST formulation
          %p
            General discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST-BV+formalism"} BRST-BV formalism
            (breaking of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+master+equation"} quantum master equation
            by quantum corrections) is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                W. Troost, P. van Nieuwenhuizen, A. van Proeyen,
                %em Anomalies and the Batalin-Vilkovisky lagrangian formalism
                (
                %a{:href => "http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1990NuPhB.333..727T"}> web
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Howe"}> Paul Howe
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulf+Lindstr%C3%B6m"} Ulf Lindström
                %em Anomalies And Renormalization In The BRST-BV Framework
                , Phys. Lett. B246 (1990) 430.
            %li
              %p
                J. Paris, W. Troost,
                %em Higher loop anomalies and their consistency conditions in nonlocal regularization
                , Nucl. Phys. B482 (1996) 373 (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9607215"}> arXiv:hep-th/9607215
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Glenn+Barnich"}> Glenn Barnich
                ,
                %em Classical and quantum aspects of the
              %p
                extended antifield formalism_ (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0011120"}> arXiv:hep-th/0011120
                )
          %p The fact that the anomaly sits in degree-1 BRST cohomology corresponds to the consistency condition discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Julius+Wess"} Julius Wess
              and B. Zumino,
              %em Consequences of anomalous Ward identities
              , Phys. Lett. B37 (1971) 95
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/0370-2693(71)90582-X"} doi
            %li
              R. Stora,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Wess Zumino consistency condition: a paradigm in renormalized perturbation theory
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1002/prop.200510266"} doi
          %p Discussion of special applications in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                F. De Jonghe, J. Paris and W. Troost,
                %em The BPHZ renormalised BV master equation and Two-loop Anomalies in Chiral Gravities
                , Nucl. Phys. B476 (1996) 559
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9502140"} arXiv:hep-th/9603012
            %li
              %p
                J. Paris,
                %em
                  Nonlocally regularized antibracket - antifield formalism and anomalies in chiral
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W(3)
                  gravity
                , Nucl. Phys. B450 (1995) 357 (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9502140"}> arXiv:hep-th/9502140
                )
            %li
              %p
                R. Amorim, N.R.F.Braga, R. Thibes,
                %em Axial and gauge anomalies in the field antifield quantization of the generalized Schwinger model
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9712014"}> arXiv:hep-th/9712014
                )
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+analysis"} functional analysis
            taken into account is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                section 5.3.3. in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"} Katarzyna Rejzner
                %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in locally covariant field theory
                PhD thesis, Hamburg (2011) (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1111.5130"}> arXiv:1111.5130
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"}> Klaus Fredenhagen
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Katarzyna+Rejzner"} Katarzyna Rejzner
                %em Batalin-Vilkovisky formalism in perturbative algebraic quantum field theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1110.5232"}> arXiv:1110.5232
                )
          %h4#other_2 Other
          %p
            An interpretation of gauge anomalies as failures of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-adjoint+extension"} self-adjoint extension
          %ul
            %li
              J. G. Esteve,
              %em Origin of the anomalies: the modified Heisenberg equation
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0207164"}> arXiv:hep-th/0207164
              )
          %h3#anomaly_inflow Anomaly inflow
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Curtis+Callan"}> Curtis Callan
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeffrey+Harvey"} Jeffrey Harvey
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Anomalies and Fermion Zero Modes on Strings and Domain Walls
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(85)90489-4"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(85)90489-4
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/15691"} spire:15691
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kazuya+Yonekura"} Kazuya Yonekura
                %em
                  Anomaly Inflow and the
                  = succeed "-Invariant" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi η
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.08775"}> arXiv:1909.08775
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/1755070"} spire:1755070
          %h3#in_finitedimensional_quantum_mechanics In finite-dimensional quantum mechanics
          %ul
            %li
              A. P. Balachandran, Amilcar R. de Queiroz,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Mixed states from anomalies
              %strong D85
              (2012) 025017
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.3898"} arxiv/1108.3898
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.85.025017"} doi
            %li
              Carlos Alcalde, Daniel Sternheimer,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Analytic vectors, anomalies and star representations
              %strong 17
              (1989), no. 2, 117–127.
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=993017"} MR90h:22012
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF00402326"} doi
              (the last section has also the field theory case)
